.docname {Walls}
.include {docs}

How vCortex turns dots into straight walls. This is the hardest step of the model: to the laser a
wall and a wardrobe both look like "a flat, upright surface" (see [Challenges](challenges.qd)).

## The idea

Do **not** look for lines in the cloud. Stand in the room, walk **outward**, and keep going until
there is nothing left. The last thing you meet is the wall.

- The robot walked inside the room, so the room is known (the [free floor](rooms.qd)).
- A desk or a wardrobe stands **in front** of the wall, so it is met first. The walk goes through
  it, because the gaps around furniture are short.
- Past the wall there is nothing: the laser does not see through it. A long empty stretch ends the
  walk.

.mermaid caption:{The two stages of the wall step.}
    flowchart LR
        FS[Free floor<br/>rooms as walked] --> A[Stage A<br/>outlines:<br/>walk out of each room]
        A --> B[Stage B<br/>walls:<br/>pair, join, snap, count]
        B --> W[Walls]
        B --> F[Furniture dots<br/>for the object step]

| Stage | In | Out | Code |
|---|---|---|---|
| **A. Outlines** | The free floor, the dots in a height band | For each room, the straight **inner faces** of the walls around it, called **sides** | `model/wall/outline.cpp` |
| **B. Walls** | The sides of all rooms | Walls with a thickness; the dots that are furniture | `model/wall/walls.cpp` |

Everything here happens on the **squared map**: the map turned so the walls run along the x and y
axes. So a wall is either "constant x" or "constant y", and there are **four ways out** of a room:
towards +x, −x, +y, −y.

![The real room with its four ways out](images/walls-real-room.svg "The room of the Overview, squared. Real data.")

## Words and sizes

| Word | Meaning | Default size |
|---|---|---|
| **Cell** | A square of the top view | 5 cm |
| **Band** | The heights the walk looks at: from `outline.band_lo` up to the wall height minus 10 cm | 0.10 m to 2.60 m |
| **Strip** | A row of 5 cells, walked out together. Their holes fill each other | 25 cm wide |
| **Walk cell** | A step of the walk, one cell | 5 cm |
| **Face** | Where the inner surface of a wall lies on the walk axis | in metres |
| **Side** | One straight wall of a room outline: its face, and where it starts and ends along it | |

Sizes in cells, from the settings: strip = 5 cells, `max_gap` = 6 cells, `max_reach` = 30 cells,
`face_depth` = 2 cells.

## Stage A: the outline of one room

**Code:** [`outline.cpp:318`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/wall/outline.cpp#L318) `find_room_outlines` · [`outline.cpp:303`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/wall/outline.cpp#L303) `find_room_outline`

Done for each room, and for each of the four ways out.

### A1. Cut the grid into strips

**Code:** [`outline.cpp:115`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/wall/outline.cpp#L115) `sample_wall_faces` · [`outline.cpp:123`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/wall/outline.cpp#L123) `strip in cells`

The grid is cut into strips across the walls, 25 cm wide. Each strip that touches the room gives
**one sample**: where along the wall the strip lies, and the face it found (or none).

### A2. Find the farthest room cell

**Code:** [`outline.cpp:50`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/wall/outline.cpp#L50) `find_outermost_room_cell`

In a strip, look from the **outer edge of the grid inwards** for the first cell that belongs to the
room. That is the farthest-out room cell. Looking from outside in means a hole in the floor (under
a table) never stops the search early.

### A3. Walk out until the wall

**Code:** [`outline.cpp:73`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/wall/outline.cpp#L73) `find_wall_cell` · [`outline.cpp:61`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/wall/outline.cpp#L61) `has_band_point`

From that cell, step outward one cell at a time, up to `max_reach` = 1.5 m. At each step, ask: does
**any** of the 5 rows of the strip hold a dot of the band?

- Yes: remember this step, and reset the count of empty cells.
- No: add one to the count of empty cells in a row. At `max_gap` = 6 (30 cm) the walk stops.
- The **wall cell** is the last step that had a dot. If there was none, or the reach ran out first:
  no wall in this strip.

**Example,** one strip, one way out. (Made-up numbers.)

![One strip walked out cell by cell, and the face found at the end](images/walls-walk-out.svg "A made-up strip: walk, stop, face.")

- The desk (cells 41–43) is met first. Then 3 empty cells: fewer than 6, so the walk goes on.
- The wall (47–48) is met. Then 6 empty cells: the walk stops.
- The wall cell is 48, the last one with dots.

If the wall itself had not been seen, the walk would have stopped after the desk and taken the
**desk** for the wall. Step A5 below is what catches most of those cases.

### A4. Read the face

**Code:** [`outline.cpp:94`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/wall/outline.cpp#L94) `find_face`

The face is the **median** of the mean positions of the dots, over the **last 2 cells** up to the
wall cell (`face_depth` = 10 cm), over all 5 rows. The lidar sees only the **inner** surface of a
wall, so those dots are the face. The narrow window keeps furniture in front of the wall out.

**Example.** Cells 47 and 48, five rows, eight cells hold dots. Their mean positions, in metres,
sorted: 2.37, 2.38, 2.38, 2.39, 2.40, 2.41, 2.41, 2.42. The median (the middle value, the upper
one for an even count) is **2.40 m**. The face of this strip is x = 2.40.

### A5. From samples to sides

**Code:** [`outline.cpp:273`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/wall/outline.cpp#L273) `find_outline_sides` · [`outline.cpp:154`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/wall/outline.cpp#L154) `split_into_segments` · [`outline.cpp:230`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/wall/outline.cpp#L230) `drop_short_segments` · [`outline.cpp:172`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/wall/outline.cpp#L172) `merge_close_segments` · [`outline.cpp:191`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/wall/outline.cpp#L191) `bridge_dents`

A wall gives a whole row of strips with about the same face. The row of samples, in order along
the wall, becomes sides in five steps:

| # | Step | Rule (default) |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | **Split** | A new piece starts when a face is more than `segment_jump` = 15 cm from the median of the piece so far. A strip with **no face** (a door) neither splits nor joins |
| 2 | **Drop short pieces** | Fewer than `min_strips_per_segment` = 2 strips: a chair leg, a bag |
| 3 | **Merge** | Neighbour pieces with faces within `merge_distance` = 10 cm become one |
| 4 | **Bridge dents** | A piece at most `dent_length` = 2.0 m long, lying 10 cm to `dent_depth` = 50 cm **inward** of a neighbour, is furniture against the wall. The neighbour grows over it. Repeated until nothing changes |
| 5 | **Merge again** | A bridge can bring two outer pieces next to each other |

A side is then: the **median face** of its strips, and its start and end along the wall.

**Example, step by step.** Strips of 25 cm; the way out is +x. Faces, in metres: 4.0 for 4
strips, 3.7 for 3 strips, 4.0 for 4 strips. A cupboard 0.75 m long, 30 cm in front of the wall.
(This is a real test case.)

| Step | Pieces after it |
|---|---|
| 1 Split | [0.00–1.00 at 4.0], [1.00–1.75 at 3.7], [1.75–2.75 at 4.0] |
| 2 Drop | all three have at least 2 strips: no change |
| 3 Merge | 4.0 and 3.7 are 0.30 apart: no change |
| 4 Bridge | The middle piece is 0.75 m ≤ 2.0 m long, and 4.0 − 3.7 = 0.30 is between 0.10 and 0.50: it is a dent. The left neighbour grows over it: [0.00–1.75 at 4.0] |
| 5 Merge | 4.0 and 4.0: one piece [0.00–2.75] |

Result: **one side**, face 4.0, from 0.00 to 2.75. The cupboard did not move the wall.

![Samples becoming one side](images/walls-sides-from-samples.svg "Steps 1 to 5 of A5 on the cupboard case.")

**Other real test cases:**

| Faces along the wall (strips of 25 cm) | Result |
|---|---|
| 4.0 for 6 strips, then 2.0 for 6 strips | **2 sides**: an L-shaped room: (4.0, 0–1.5) and (2.0, 1.5–3.0) |
| 4.0 for 4, **no face** for 4, 4.0 for 4 | **1 side**: (4.0, 0–3.0). A door does not split a wall |
| 4.0 for 4, 3.7 for **10**, 4.0 for 4 | **3 sides**: the thing is 2.5 m long, over `dent_length` = 2.0 m, so it counts as a wall of its own |

### A6. Join the corners

**Code:** [`outline.cpp:290`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/wall/outline.cpp#L290) `join_outline_corners` · [`outline.cpp:248`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/wall/outline.cpp#L248) `find_corner`

The seen part of a wall often stops short of its corners, because the view was blocked there. Each
end of a side is moved to the **face of the nearest perpendicular side** that faces out the way the
end heads, when **both** reach the other's face:

- short of it by up to `corner_max_extension` = 8 m (stretch), or
- past it by up to `corner_overshoot` = 0.5 m (trim back).

Otherwise the end stays where it was seen.

**Examples, real test cases.** East side: face x = 4.0, seen from y = 0.3 to 2.7. North side: face
y = 3.0, seen from x = 0.2 to 3.6.

| Case | What happens |
|---|---|
| The east side stops 0.3 short of the north face, the north side 0.4 short of the east face | Both reach each other: east ends at y = **3.0**, north at x = **4.0** |
| The east side pokes past: seen to y = 3.4 | 0.4 past is under 0.5: trimmed back to **3.0** |
| East side seen from 0.3 to 1.7; the north wall runs on to x = 6.0, 2.0 past the east face | A **T**, not a corner: the east end stays at **1.7** |

The result does not depend on the order of the sides.

![Three corner cases](images/walls-corners.svg "Stretch, trim, and a T that is not a corner.")

## Stage B: from sides to walls

All sides of all rooms go in together.

### B1. Pair the two faces of a wall

**Code:** [`walls.cpp:35`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/wall/walls.cpp#L35) `find_other_face` · [`walls.cpp:58`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/wall/walls.cpp#L58) `make_pieces` · [`walls.cpp:17`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/wall/walls.cpp#L17) `kPairMin` · [`walls.cpp:18`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/wall/walls.cpp#L18) `kPairMax`

A wall between two rooms is a side in **each** outline, facing back to back. Two sides make **one
wall** when they:

- are on the same axis, facing opposite ways;
- overlap along the wall;
- lie **5 to 35 cm** apart (fixed numbers in the code, not settings).

Then the centre line is **halfway** and the thickness is **their distance**. If several fit, the
nearest wins. A side with no partner is a wall seen from **one room only**: its thickness is the
setting `walls.thickness` = 10 cm, and its centre line lies half of that **behind** the face.

**Example, real test.** Left room: east face at x = 1.70. Right room: west face at x = 1.82.
Distance 0.12, so one wall: centre **1.76**, thickness **0.12**. The same face seen alone: centre
1.70 + 0.05 = **1.75**, thickness **0.10**.

### B2. Join pieces on one line

**Code:** [`walls.cpp:89`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/wall/walls.cpp#L89) `join_collinear`

Pieces on one line (same axis, same side of the room, centre lines within
`collinear_tolerance` = 10 cm) are joined across gaps up to `min_gap` = 30 cm. The centre line and
the thickness of the result are the averages, **weighted by length**. A longer gap stays open: a
door, or wall never seen.

**Example.** Piece 1: from 0 to 2.0 m, centre 3.00. Piece 2: from 2.2 to 4.0 m, centre 3.02. The gap
is 0.2 m, so one wall from 0 to 4.0 m, centre (3.00 × 2.0 + 3.02 × 1.8) ÷ 3.8 = **3.009**. With a gap
of 0.9 m they stay two walls. (Made-up numbers.)

![Pairing two faces, one face, and joining pieces](images/walls-pair-faces.svg "Stage B: faces become walls.")

### B3. Snap the corners

**Code:** [`walls.cpp:143`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/wall/walls.cpp#L143) `close_corners`

Each end of a wall moves to the **centre line** of a perpendicular wall, if that one is within
`corner_gap` = 50 cm. So the walls of a room meet exactly.

### B4. Sort and name

**Code:** [`walls.cpp:211`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/wall/walls.cpp#L211) `sort` · [`walls.cpp:218`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/wall/walls.cpp#L218) `id`

Walls are sorted by axis (walls of constant x come first), then by centre line, then by start. They
are numbered `wall-1`, `wall-2`, … in that order.

### B5. Count the dots, and keep the furniture

**Code:** [`walls.cpp:227`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/wall/walls.cpp#L227) `dot loop` · [`walls.cpp:191`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/wall/walls.cpp#L191) `distance_from_point_to_wall` · [`walls.cpp:178`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/wall/walls.cpp#L178) `is_inside_any_outline`

Each dot in the band (from `candidates.band_lo` = 30 cm to the wall height minus 10 cm) is sorted:

| The dot is | It becomes |
|---|---|
| Within `object_clearance` = 10 cm of a wall's body (centre line ± half the thickness, along its length) | A dot **of that wall** (the nearest wall wins). It adds 1 to `points` |
| Not near a wall, but inside a room outline | **Furniture**: it goes to the [object step](objects.qd) |
| Past every outline | **Dropped**: it is outside |

## Real numbers

The room of the [Overview](introduction.qd). The map was turned by 16.8°. These are the walls in
the **squared** map, worked back from the twin file (centre lines, in metres).

| Wall | Constant | Runs from … to | Length | Dots |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `wall-1` | x = −6.68 | y = −3.42 to 3.12 | 6.55 m | 761 |
| `wall-2` | x = 1.84 | y = −3.43 to 3.12 | 6.55 m | 1,392 |
| `wall-3` | y = −3.42 | x = −6.68 to 1.84 | 8.52 m | 1,533 |
| `wall-4` | y = 3.12 | x = −6.68 to 1.84 | 8.52 m | 1,511 |

- **1 room, 4 outline sides**: one side per way out, as the report says.
- **Walls 1 and 2 are constant x**, walls 3 and 4 constant y: the naming rule of B4.
- **Corners are snapped (B3)**: the ends of `wall-1` are at y = −3.42 and 3.12, exactly the centre
  lines of `wall-3` and `wall-4`.
- **Thickness is 0.10 m for all four.** That is the default, not a measure: each wall is seen from
  one room only (B1). Working back, the inner faces lie at x = −6.63 and 1.79, y = −3.37 and 3.07.
- **5,197 dots** belong to walls. **71,262** dots went on as furniture, into the 19 objects.

## The settings of this chapter

| Group | Settings | See |
|---|---|---|
| `outline` | `band_lo`, `strip`, `max_reach`, `max_gap`, `face_depth`, `segment_jump`, `min_strips_per_segment`, `merge_distance`, `dent_depth`, `dent_length`, `corner_max_extension`, `corner_overshoot` | A1 to A6 |
| `walls` | `collinear_tolerance`, `min_gap`, `door_max`, `corner_gap`, `thickness`, `object_clearance` | B1 to B5 |
| `candidates` | `band_lo`, `band_top_margin` | B5 and the band of stage A |

`walls.door_max` is not used in this chapter; it is used by the [final rooms](rooms.qd). All the
values are in [Settings](settings.qd).

